---
import "../styles/app.css";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Chia PoS2 Browser Plotter</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header>
      <h1>Chia PoS2 Browser Plotter</h1>
      <p class="sub">
        Proof of Space v2 plots (<code>.plot2</code>) made in your browser with WebAssembly.
        Format matches <code>chia plots create2</code> (chia_rs 0.49, pos2-chip 0.6.0). Alpha.
        Chia does not accept v2 proofs yet: they activate with a future hard fork, so these plots
        pass <code>chia plots check</code> but cannot win blocks until then.
      </p>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="card" id="env">
        <h2>Browser</h2>
        <ul id="env-list"></ul>
      </section>

      <form class="card" id="config">
        <h2>Plot settings</h2>

        <label>
          Farmer public key
          <input name="farmerKey" required autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"
            placeholder="96 hex characters, from `chia keys show`" />
          <span class="err" data-for="farmerKey"></span>
        </label>

        <fieldset>
          <legend>Pool</legend>
          <label class="inline"><input type="radio" name="poolMode" value="contract" checked /> Pool contract address (NFT plot)</label>
          <label class="inline"><input type="radio" name="poolMode" value="pk" /> Pool public key (solo, OG)</label>
          <label data-mode="contract">
            Pool contract address
            <input name="poolAddress" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="xch1… from `chia plotnft show`" />
            <span class="err" data-for="poolAddress"></span>
          </label>
          <label data-mode="pk" hidden>
            Pool public key
            <input name="poolKey" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="96 hex characters" />
            <span class="err" data-for="poolKey"></span>
          </label>
        </fieldset>

        <div class="row">
          <label>
            Strength
            <input name="strength" type="number" min="2" max="17" value="2" />
            <small>2 to 17. Each step halves how often the plot passes the filter, and consensus doubles its chance to win when it passes, so expected rewards are the same at any strength. Higher means fewer disk reads but slower plotting and proving. The filter caps at 1 in 8,192 today, reached at strength 6. Higher strengths only help after the <a href="https://github.com/Chia-Network/chips/pull/161" target="_blank" rel="noopener">scheduled</a> changes: base filter cuts (first one 6 years after the fork, then every 3 years) and later rises in the minimum strength, after which plots at the old minimum must be replotted.</small>
            <small id="read-rate" class="read-rate"></small>
          </label>
          <label>
            Size (k)
            <select name="k">
              <option value="28" selected>k28 (network size, about 1 GB file, needs about 11 GB of memory)</option>
              <option value="26">k26 (test only, about 230 MB)</option>
              <option value="24">k24 (test only, about 53 MB)</option>
              <option value="22">k22 (test only, about 12 MB)</option>
              <option value="20">k20 (test only)</option>
              <option value="18">k18 (test only)</option>
            </select>
            <small>Farming requires k28. Smaller sizes are for testing the pipeline.</small>
          </label>
        </div>

        <fieldset>
          <legend>Plot groups</legend>
          <details class="explain">
            <summary>Explain plot groups and meta groups</summary>
            <h3>What problem groups solve</h3>
            <p>
              Every 9 to 10 seconds the network announces a signage point (64 per 10 minute sub
              slot, 9,216 a day). For each one, every plot runs a quick filter, and only plots that
              pass have to be read from disk. At strength 2 a plot passes about 1 time in 512.
            </p>
            <p>
              With no groups, every plot passes at its own random times. A 4 TB drive holds about
              4,000 k28 plots, so on average about 8 of them pass at every signage point. The drive
              is read every few seconds, all day, and can never spin down.
            </p>
            <p>
              A <b>plot group</b> is a set of plots that share one plot key. The filter is decided
              for the group as a whole, so all of its plots pass together, at the same signage
              points, and at the same rate as a single plot. Put one group on a drive and the drive
              only has to be read when that group passes. Grouping does not change expected
              rewards: each plot still passes as often as before, the passes just happen together.
            </p>
            <h3>How often a group passes</h3>
            <table class="mini">
              <thead><tr><th>Strength</th><th>Passes per signage point</th><th>Wakeups per day</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>2</td><td>1 in 512</td><td>about 18</td></tr>
                <tr><td>3</td><td>1 in 1,024</td><td>about 9</td></tr>
                <tr><td>4</td><td>1 in 2,048</td><td>about 4.5</td></tr>
                <tr><td>5</td><td>1 in 4,096</td><td>about 2.3</td></tr>
                <tr><td>6 and up</td><td>1 in 8,192 (current cap)</td><td>about 1.1</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <p>
              These are today's rates. Chia has <a href="https://github.com/Chia-Network/chips/pull/161" target="_blank" rel="noopener">scheduled</a>
              cuts to the base filter (proposed in CHIP-49: the first 6 years after the fork, then
              every 3 years), which make every strength pass more often, so higher strengths keep
              drives quieter for longer. CHIP-49 also plans to raise the minimum strength later
              (not for 10 or more years, with months of warning), and plots at the old minimum then
              have to be replotted. CHIP-49 counts strength from 0, while chia-blockchain and this
              page count from 2, so CHIP strength 4 is strength 6 here. Higher strength costs plotting time (strength 6 took about 3.5 times as long
              as strength 2 in our tests) and makes each proof slower to solve, so check your
              hardware with Validate plots before committing a lot of plots to a high strength.
            </p>
            <h3>What the meta group is</h3>
            <p>
              Think of it as a sub-group. Chia's <a href="https://github.com/Chia-Network/chips/pull/161" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CHIP-49</a>
              nests three levels: a <b>group ID</b> (one plot key, strength and pool) holds up to 256
              <b>meta groups</b>, and each meta group holds up to 65,536 plots by <b>plot index</b>.
              Plots in the same group ID and meta group pass the filter together, which is what this
              page calls a plot group. The name meta group is the protocol's own term
              (<code>meta_group</code>), and the CHIPs do not explain the choice of word.
            </p>
            <p>
              Signage points are grouped into windows of 16, and a group passes at most once per
              window. The <b>meta group</b> is a number from 0 to 255 stored in each plot that
              shifts <i>when</i> a group passes, but only compared with other groups that share the same
              plot key:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>Meta groups in different blocks of 16 (0 to 15, 16 to 31, 32 to 47, …) never pass in the same window. 15 and 16 are in different blocks.</li>
              <li>Meta groups in the same block of 16 pass in the same windows, at different signage points.</li>
              <li>Groups with different plot keys pass at unrelated times, so their meta groups do not matter.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              So the group ID comes first and the meta group only subdivides it: the meta group
              has no meaning except relative to other plots with the same plot key and strength.
              In CHIP-49's words, plots in a group attract each other and meta groups repel each
              other. CHIP-49 recommends giving each plot group on the same drive its own meta group,
              which spreads out the drive's reads and the harvester's CPU work.
            </p>
            <p>
              One limit: the filter only looks at as many bits as its current difficulty. The
              blocks of 16 stay distinct while the filter uses 8 or more bits, which is true today
              at every strength (9 to 13 bits). When Chia lowers the base filter later, low
              strength plots drop below 8 bits and blocks start to coincide (at 6 bits, meta groups
              0 and 64 pass together). Strength 6 keeps 8 or more bits through the first 5 planned
              reductions.
            </p>
            <p>
              That is what the staggered option uses: up to 16 groups (16 drives) share one plot
              key and get meta groups 0, 16, 32, …, so no two of those drives are needed in the same
              window, as long as the filter uses 8 or more bits.
            </p>
            <h3>Example setups</h3>
            <dl>
              <dt>One 4 TB drive that should sleep</dt>
              <dd>New groups, independent, 4,000 plots per group (the whole drive is one group),
                strength 4 to 6. The drive is needed about 1 to 5 times a day.</dd>
              <dt>Several drives on one USB hub or a weak power supply</dt>
              <dd>New groups, staggered, plots per group set to one drive's worth (4,000 for 4 TB),
                one group per drive, and turn on group subfolders so each group lands in its own
                folder. No two of the drives (up to 16) are needed in the same window, so at most one
                has to be awake at a time.</dd>
              <dt>A drive that is half full of a group</dt>
              <dd>Add to an existing group, choose that drive's group folder, and the new plots
                continue at the next free index.</dd>
              <dt>An always on NAS or SSDs</dt>
              <dd>No groups. Sleeping is not a goal, and this is the same
                as <code>chia plots create2</code>.</dd>
            </dl>
            <p>
              Pass times are also predictable: the filter for a window uses a challenge from a sub
              slot that has already finished, so a harvester can know a few minutes ahead when a
              group will be needed. Whether a drive actually sleeps depends on its own power
              settings and on the harvester, which this page does not control.
            </p>
          </details>
          <label class="inline"><input type="radio" name="groupKeys" value="none" checked /> No groups: every plot has its own key and passes the filter on its own, like <code>chia plots create2</code></label>
          <label class="inline"><input type="radio" name="groupKeys" value="separate" /> New groups, independent: each group gets its own plot key</label>
          <label class="inline"><input type="radio" name="groupKeys" value="shared" /> New groups, staggered: groups share one plot key, meta groups step by 16</label>
          <label class="inline"><input type="radio" name="groupKeys" value="continue" /> Add to an existing group</label>
          <p id="group-rate" class="group-rate" data-group-modes="separate shared continue"></p>
          <div id="continue-panel" class="subpanel" hidden>
            <p class="hint">
              A group is identified by its plot key, which is stored in every plot of the group.
              Choose the group's folder (subfolders are searched) so the page can see which plot
              indexes are taken, or a single plot from the group. New plots get the group's
              farmer key, pool, strength and meta group, and the next free indexes.
            </p>
            <div class="actions">
              <button type="button" id="cont-dir" class="secondary">Choose the group's folder…</button>
              <label class="button secondary">Choose one plot…<input id="cont-file" type="file" accept=".plot2" hidden /></label>
            </div>
            <label id="cont-pick-label" hidden>
              Group
              <select id="cont-pick"></select>
            </label>
            <p id="cont-info" class="hint"></p>
            <label>
              First new plot index
              <input id="cont-start" type="number" min="0" max="65535" value="0" />
              <small>Indexes already seen in the chosen folder are skipped. With a single plot the page cannot see the rest of the group, so set this past the highest index you have used. A reused index makes a duplicate plot.</small>
            </label>
          </div>
          <div data-group-modes="separate shared" class="subpanel">
          <label>
            Plots per group
            <input name="groupSize" type="number" min="1" max="65535" value="8" />
            <small>Plots in a group pass the filter as a unit, at the same signage points, so a drive holding one group only has to wake when that group passes. A typical choice is the number of plots that fit on one drive.</small>
          </label>
          <p class="hint">
            Staggered groups share one plot key and get meta groups 16 apart, so no two of them
            (up to 16) are needed in the same window while the filter uses 8 or more bits, which
            is true today. Explain above has the details and worked examples.
          </p>
          <label>
            Meta group of the first group (a sub-group of the plot key)
            <input name="metaGroup" type="number" min="0" max="255" value="0" />
            <small>0 to 255, stored in each plot. It only matters between groups with the same plot key: values in different blocks of 16 (0 to 15, 16 to 31, …) never share a window, values in the same block share windows but pass at different signage points. For independent groups leave it at 0.</small>
          </label>
          </div>
        </fieldset>

        <div class="row">
          <label>
            Number of plots
            <input name="count" type="number" min="0" value="1" />
            <small>0 keeps plotting until you stop.</small>
          </label>
          <label>
            Threads
            <input name="threads" type="number" min="1" />
          </label>
          <label>
            Network
            <select name="network">
              <option value="mainnet" selected>mainnet</option>
              <option value="testnet">testnet (testnet11)</option>
            </select>
          </label>
        </div>

        <fieldset>
          <legend>Output</legend>
          <label class="inline"><input type="radio" name="output" value="dir" /> Save into a folder <small id="dir-name"></small></label>
          <label class="inline"><input type="radio" name="output" value="download" /> Download each plot</label>
          <button type="button" id="pick-dir" class="secondary">Choose folder…</button>
          <p class="hint" id="dir-hint">
            Use a folder just for plots, for example a <code>plots</code> folder on the drive you
            farm from. Chrome won't open top level folders such as your home folder, Desktop,
            Documents, or Downloads, or any system folder, but folders inside them are fine.
            Use <b>New Folder</b> in the picker to make one. Then tell your harvester about it
            with <code>chia plots add -d &lt;folder&gt;</code>.
          </p>
          <label class="inline" data-group-modes="separate shared continue">
            <input type="checkbox" name="groupFolders" />
            Put each plot group in its own subfolder, <code>group-&lt;group id&gt;-m&lt;meta group&gt;</code>
          </label>
        </fieldset>

        <label>
          GPU acceleration
          <select name="gpu">
            <option value="on" selected>Use WebGPU when available</option>
            <option value="off">CPU only</option>
          </select>
          <small>Runs the g(x) hash on the GPU. Each plot first checks the GPU against the CPU on a sample, and falls back to the CPU if they differ or anything fails. Plots are identical either way.</small>
        </label>

        <label>
          Check each new plot
          <input name="autoChecks" type="number" min="0" max="1000" value="10" />
          <small>Challenges to run against each finished plot, like <code>chia plots check -n</code>. 0 turns checking off. Checks run alongside the next plot.</small>
        </label>

        <div class="actions">
          <button type="submit" id="start">Start plotting</button>
          <button type="button" id="stop" class="danger" disabled>Stop</button>
        </div>
        <p class="err" id="form-error"></p>
      </form>

      <section class="card" id="status" hidden>
        <h2>Current plot</h2>
        <div class="bar"><div id="bar-fill"></div></div>
        <p id="status-text"></p>
      </section>

      <section class="card">
        <h2>Live stats</h2>
        <div id="stats" class="stats"></div>
        <p class="hint">Web pages cannot see free system memory, other apps, or disk space, so
          this shows what the browser exposes.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="card">
        <h2>Finished plots</h2>
        <table id="history">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>File</th><th>Size</th><th title="Strength, plot group ID, index in group, meta group. Plots with the same group ID pass the filter together.">Group</th><th>Plot</th><th>Write</th><th>Total</th><th>g(x)</th><th>Check</th><th></th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody></tbody>
        </table>
      </section>
      <section class="card" id="validate">
        <h2>Validate plots</h2>
        <p class="hint">
          Check existing <code>.plot2</code> files with the same steps as <code>chia plots check</code>:
          look up proofs for each challenge, solve them, and
          validate them. Also checks that the keys in the plot produce its plot ID. Files are
          read in place in your browser. Nothing is uploaded.
        </p>
        <div class="row">
          <label>
            Challenges
            <input id="v-challenges" type="number" min="1" max="1000" value="30" />
            <small>Same as <code>chia plots check -n</code>. Default 30.</small>
          </label>
          <label>
            Network
            <select id="v-network">
              <option value="auto" selected>auto detect</option>
              <option value="mainnet">mainnet</option>
              <option value="testnet">testnet</option>
            </select>
          </label>
        </div>
        <div class="actions">
          <label class="button secondary">Choose plot files…<input id="v-files" type="file" accept=".plot2" multiple hidden /></label>
          <button type="button" id="v-dir" class="secondary">Check a folder…</button>
          <small>Folders are searched recursively, including subfolders.</small>
        </div>
        <table id="v-results">
          <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>k</th><th>Strength</th><th>Group</th><th>Index</th><th>Meta</th><th>Proofs</th><th>Keys</th><th>Name</th><th>Farmer</th><th>Time</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody></tbody>
        </table>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer>
      Keys never leave this page. The plot secret key is generated locally and stored in the plot
      memo, the same as <code>chia plots create2</code>.
    </footer>

    <script>
      import { start } from "../lib/app";
      start();
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
